[b]◈ THE YOKE & HORN[/b]
*line_break
Beside the wide stone hearth sits a heavy-set man in his early forties wearing a dark wool coat trimmed with good brown fur, his boots cleaner than anyone else's in Oxworth. Two thick-necked hired drovers sit at the end of his bench with heavy clubs leaning against their knees.

He is slapping his palm on the oak table while @{(met_sigrin) Sigrin|the dwarf innkeeper} ignores him from behind the counter. "Forty silver marks that red bull cost me in Hartwell!" he snaps. "And the village head tells me his boys aren't paid to climb down into sinkholes. Useless country."

He notices you standing by the bench and looks you up and down. "You look like someone who works for coin instead of speeches," he says loudly. "Daven Marsh, cattle buyer and tax collector out of Hartwell. Want six silver marks for an afternoon's work?"

"What happened to your bull?" you ask.

Marsh takes a swallow of ale and wipes his mouth. "Something big came up out of a sinkhole in the south pasture yesterday. Tore the belly out of my breeding bull and chased my drover off on foot. Worst of it is, my leather saddlebag dropped right beside the dead bull."

He leans forward, tapping one fat finger on the table. "Six silver marks if you kill whatever lives in that pit, bring me a piece of it so I know it's dead, and bring back my saddlebag with the lock untouched. No peeking inside. Just my business papers from Hartwell, and I pay people to carry, not read."

At the foot of the corner stair, a tall woman in her late twenties watches him over the rim of a clay wine cup. She wears dark green wool that makes the smoky room look twice as grey, her dark hair pinned loose over one shoulder and a sharp, bored look in her eyes.

"And stay away from my wife while you're in this house," Marsh adds with a loud laugh, not even glancing back at the stairs. "She's only out here in the mud because men in Hartwell don't know how to keep their eyes to themselves. Well? Do we have a deal?"

