[b]◈ THE YOKE & HORN: CART YARD[/b]
*line_break
As you step out toward the cart yard for air, the tall woman from the stairs slips out behind you, pulling the wooden door shut to keep the noise of the taproom inside. Up close, she is not frightened or beaten down at all. She has dark, amused eyes, a clean jawline, and the relaxed posture of someone who has been bored for three days and just found a game worth playing.

"Vera Marsh," she says smoothly, leaning one shoulder against the stone wall beside the water trough. "And before you look sorry for me, don't. I married Daven for a stone house in Hartwell, not for his manners."

"You want something from me," you say plainly.

Her mouth curves in a slow smirk. "Two things, maybe. First, free advice. Daven told you not to open his saddlebag down in the sinkholes. Open it anyway. He has two tax books inside. One honest list for Hartwell's counting-house, and a second list where he charges Oxworth herders double as foreign stock and pockets the difference. @{(met_ox_head) Bram Tallow|The village head across the lane} would pay good coin for that book. Or you can hold it under Daven's nose and bleed him for twice what he promised you."

"Why hand me a knife to stick in your own husband?" you ask.

"Because he dragged me out to a mud village to punish me for smiling at someone in Hartwell, and then left me upstairs alone while he drinks and throws dice every night," Vera says, her voice dropping warm and low. She steps a pace closer, smelling of cedar soap and red wine. "Which brings me to the second thing. After dark, Daven sits at the hearth dice table until midnight. Our room is the end door at the top of the corner stair. If you want to make his stay in Oxworth really memorable, knock once when the dice come out."

She gives you one slow look from head to toe, turns on her heel, and heads back inside before Daven even notices she stepped out.
